WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
Backup jobs often need broad read access and sometimes write access to protected storage.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.440
That does not mean they should run as an all-powerful account by default.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.020
Least privilege still applies.

4
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.000
A backup identity should have the access required for its specific job and no more.

5
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.900
The account that reads source data may not need permission to delete historical backups.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.840
The identity that writes to backup storage may not need administrative control over every source system.

7
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.880
Dedicated backup identities help make access review possible.

8
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.500
A shared administrator account hides purpose and ownership.

9
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:44.360
A dedicated identity can be tied to a system, dataset, schedule, storage destination, and recovery requirement.

10
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:51.340
It can have scoped credentials, monitored activity, clear rotation procedures, and a defined revocation path.

11
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:57.920
If the backup job is retired, the identity can be retired with it rather than remaining as hidden privileged access.

12
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.080
Separate read, write, delete, and administrative permissions.

13
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:11.500
A script may need to read production data and write a new backup object, but it may not need to overwrite previous backups or change storage policy.

14
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.840
Deletion rights should be especially carefully controlled because cleanup mistakes can damage recovery capability.

15
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:25.220
Where possible, use storage controls, roles, or separate processes to prevent one script from having every possible capability.

16
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:31.900
Secrets used for storage, encryption, transfer, or service access should be protected.

17
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.880
Keep them out of script files, command histories, logs, and broad environment dumps.

18
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:41.300
Use approved secret-management mechanisms or controlled runtime injection.

19
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.120
Review file ownership and permissions for local configuration and key material.

20
00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:52.940
Rotate credentials on a schedule appropriate to the environment and revoke them promptly when a workflow changes or exposure is suspected.
